{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgByGmG7C743buk6ZV2Pz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Opening a modal by name:** the caller imports `showModal` from `@openmrs/esm-framework` and passes a registered modal name, optional props, and an optional `onClose` callback. It returns a `close` function. The name must have been registered in the `modals` section of the app's `routes.json`. Registration goes through `registerAppRoutes` → `tryRegisterModal` → `registerModal`. Unknown names are reported through `reportError`, and the call does nothing else.\n\n**Closing it:** the caller calls the returned function. It finds the stack entry whose `onClose` matches and marks it `TO_BE_DELETED`. The store subscriber then runs `onClose`, unmounts the parcel, and removes the frame. The modal component also receives `close` in its props, so it can close itself. The Escape key closes the topmost modal through `closeHighestInstance`.\n\n**Example callers:**\n- `change-password-link.extension.tsx` keeps the return value as `dispose` and passes `closeModal: () => dispose()` in props, so the modal can close itself.\n- `two-factor-auth.component.tsx` opens `totp-enrollment-modal` and ignores the return value.\n\n**Documentation:** `showModal.md` (generated from the source JSDoc) and the JSDoc on `showModal` itself both describe the registration requirement and the return value. `API.md` links to the `showModal` page.\n\n**Caveat:** `openInstance` spreads `...props` after `close`, so a caller passing its own `close` prop would override the framework's. The example callers use `closeModal` to avoid this.\n\nI did not run any tests; this is from reading the source.\n\n**Declarations, best first:**\n\n1. `openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-styleguide/src/modals/index.tsx` lines 280–319: `showModal`, the main entry point and its JSDoc.\n2. `openmrs-esm-core/packages/framework/esm-styleguide/src/modals/index.tsx` li